E_{1}\left(p,q\odot r\right)=E_{1}\left(E_{1}(p,q),r\right); in particular, if q is multiplicatively idempotent, E_{1}(p,q)=E_{1}\left(E_{1}(p,q),q\right);

E_{2}\left(p,E_{2}(q,r)\right)=E_{2}\left(p\odot q,r\right); in particular, if p is multiplicatively idempotent, E_{2}\left(p,E_{2}(p,q)\right)=E_{2}(p,q);


All these proofs consist, basically, of straightforward checks on the definitions.
(1) Let A\subseteq \mathbb {N}. Then

A\in E_{1}\left(p^{a},q\right)\Leftrightarrow \lbrace n\in \mathbb {N}\mid \lbrace m\in \mathbb {N}\mid n^{m}\in A\rbrace \in q\rbrace \in p^{a}\Leftrightarrow \\ \lbrace n\in \mathbb {N}\mid n^{a}\in \lbrace l\in \mathbb {N}\mid \lbrace m\in \mathbb {N}\mid l^{m}\in A\rbrace \in q\rbrace \rbrace \in p\Leftrightarrow \lbrace n\in \mathbb {N}\mid \lbrace m\in \mathbb {N}\mid \left(n^{a}\right)^{m}\in A\rbrace \in q\rbrace \in p\Leftrightarrow \\ \lbrace n\in \mathbb {N}\mid \lbrace m\in \mathbb {N}\mid \left(n^{am}\right)\in A\rbrace \in q\rbrace \in p\Leftrightarrow \lbrace n\in \mathbb {N}\mid \lbrace m\in \mathbb {N}\mid \left(n^{m}\right)\in A\rbrace \in aq\rbrace \in p\Leftrightarrow \\ A\in E_{1}\left(p,aq\right).
The E_{2}-case is analogous.

